From: PS5_Proton <local>
Subject: [PATCH] gpu-broker: the broker keeps its staging and takes it again, making and freeing none during play

In a 2026-10-16 run (a Unity game in the earlier emulator experiment (removed), build 57ead6f6f8514184; record removed from the tree; in git history) the title's flight recorder found the
broker's client lock held from t=34 to t=48 (busy:1 to busy:15) in phase reap-free, the free of a submission's staging
once its fence passed (0017's phase record), with fences 8125/8125 (the GPU had finished everything) and flips +0. In
the same seconds the game's helper wrote a stall line every second (held_ms=2829 to 14900): one thread held Wine's
virtual mutex and the direct-memory engine's lock at site 0x8004d8adb, NtProtectVirtualMemory, that is
__wine_ps5_mprotect, pw_wine_dmem_protect and sceKernelMprotect of direct memory (the emulator's write watches), in the
kernel. Both began within about 0.1 s of each other (about t=33.1). At 15 s the helper's GPU client gave the broker up
("no answer in 15 s (operation 4 sequence 9378)"), the emulator's vkQueueSubmit returned ErrorDeviceLost and it exited, the
helper ended, and only then did the free return (t=49, fences 8126/8126) and the console recover. The same device loss
ended two 2026-10-15 runs at about 150 s and at about 37 s, whose console then froze.

The broker made a staging buffer (radv_ps5_memory_alloc) for every submission and freed it once its fence passed:
8126 submissions in that session, about 420 of each a second while the game played (fences 6842 to 8100 from t=30 to
t=33), each a direct-memory allocation or release in the title while the helper changed the protection of direct
memory.

Now reap keeps a buffer whose fence has passed as a spare, and submit takes a spare of its own rounded size, with no
kernel call (staging.h, bookkeeping only, host-tested in PS5_Proton tests/test_pw_gpu_staging.c). Sizes are rounded to
64 KiB at least, powers of two to 2 MiB and 2 MiB steps above (submissions averaged about 4 KiB: the helper's radv/ps5
line, 2350 submissions, 2442 kwords), and a buffer is made only when every one of its size is in flight, so per size no
more are made than were once in flight together. A submission never takes a bigger spare: taking the smallest spare
that fits (this patch's first form, replacing the largest spare when none fit) let small submissions hold the large
spares, so the next large submission made another, and in the host model below the spares grew to about 262 MiB in
10 minutes and 279 MiB in an hour (14 buffers of 18 MiB), the title's direct memory, held until the broker stopped,
which the broker's 1 GiB local floor would have taken from clients' device-local room. The spares also hold at most
32 MiB (PS5_GPU_STAGING_BUDGET: the fence ring's 512 at 64 KiB, or one 18 MiB buffer for a 16 MiB submission and
14 MiB of others): reap frees a buffer past it (phase reap-free), and a staging never submitted (a refused tess or
offchip state change) goes back as a spare or is freed past it (phase stage-free). Otherwise spares are freed only in
fences_reset, where the fence page goes back after the stop's drain; wedged, nothing is freed, as before. The stop
writes "gpu-broker staging allocs= frees= reuses= refused= peak_bytes= held_bytes=" before the dmem_host line:
allocations few, reuses near the submissions, refused (frees during play) zero, and allocs equal to frees after a clean
stop. The counters are printed only at the stop, so a run that freezes does not report them; the budget bounds what it
can hold. On the host, 252,000 submissions of that shape (4 KiB mostly, 1% up to 1 MiB, 0.01% at the 16 MiB limit) with
1 to 40 in flight made 51 buffers, took a spare 251,949 times and refused none; the spares held 26,542,080 bytes
(25.3 MiB, one buffer of 18 MiB) at the end, also their peak with what was in flight. An hour of it (1,512,000) made
53 and held 28,114,944 bytes.

What it is not: it removes the title's side of one collision, not the kernel's cause. Why the title's release of its
own direct memory waited while another process's sceKernelMprotect of direct memory was held up is not known, and it is
not shown that the free waited on that call rather than both on something else. The title still releases and makes
direct memory during play elsewhere: reap's release of deferred client buffers (release(fences.deferred[i].buffer),
unchanged: a device-local buffer's radv_ps5_memory_free, a host range laid back, dmem_give_back), free_buffer's
immediate release, and client allocations. Those follow the game's buffers, not its submissions (that session's
dmem_host line: 35 host-visible buffers made and 35 freed; device-local ones are not counted), and are the next
suspects if the stall recurs; the phase record names them (munmap, give-back, alloc). Not run on the console.
---
diff --git a/tooling/gpu-broker/server.cpp b/tooling/gpu-broker/server.cpp
index 2446cc9..8662fff 100644
--- a/tooling/gpu-broker/server.cpp
+++ b/tooling/gpu-broker/server.cpp
@@ -11,6 +11,7 @@
 
 extern "C" {
 #include "radv_ps5_platform.h"
+#include "staging.h"
 #include "protocol.h"
 #include "frame_page.h"
 #include "pw_window_scene.h"
@@ -435,9 +436,9 @@ int allocate(Client &c,PwGpuMessage &m,uint64_t &next_window,uint64_t &next_high
 // Asynchronous submission. The broker copies a client's stream into owner-private staging, appends its own
 // end-of-work packet writing a sequence number to an owner-private fence page, submits, and replies at once: the
 // client waits on its own marker, which the GPU's writes reach coherently (graphics-child-gpu-coherence-*). Every
-// client's work goes to the one GPU ring in submission order, so one fence orders it all. Staging, and the backing of
-// any buffer a client frees while work is in flight, are released only once the fence has passed the last
-// submission that could use them.
+// client's work goes to the one GPU ring in submission order, so one fence orders it all. A submission's staging is
+// kept as a spare once the fence has passed it and taken again by a later one (staging.h); the backing of any buffer a
+// client frees while work is in flight is released only once the fence has passed the last submission that could use it.
 struct Inflight { uint32_t seq;radv_ps5_memory staging; };
 struct Deferred { Buffer buffer;uint32_t seq; };
 struct Fences {
@@ -446,6 +447,7 @@ struct Fences {
     static constexpr unsigned kRing=512,kDeferred=1024;
     Inflight ring[kRing]{};unsigned head=0,count=0;
     Deferred deferred[kDeferred]{};unsigned deferred_count=0;
+    ps5_gpu_staging staging{};
     // The GPU state every client shares, as last set: changing it waits for all work in flight.
     uint64_t tess=0;uint32_t tess_bytes=0,granularity=0,buffering=0;bool offchip=false;
     uint64_t drains=0,state_drains=0;
@@ -453,6 +455,14 @@ struct Fences {
     // replies are out, rather than by the platform's kick thread inside the round trip.
     bool kick=false;uint64_t kicks=0,kick_ns=0;
 } fences;
+static_assert(PS5_GPU_STAGING_SPARES>=Fences::kRing,"the spares' slots hold what the ring held; only the budget refuses");
+// Staging made, freed, taken again, and given back past the spares' budget (each a free during play); the bytes made and
+// not freed, and their peak. Across broker restarts, as dmem_host's.
+unsigned long long staging_allocs=0,staging_frees=0,staging_reuses=0,staging_refused=0,staging_held=0,staging_peak=0;
+void staging_free(radv_ps5_memory &memory)
+{
+    staging_held-=memory.bytes;++staging_frees;radv_ps5_memory_free(&memory);
+}
 int broker_submit(void *words,uint32_t count)
 {
     radv_ps5_cpu_flush(words,size_t(count)*4);
@@ -481,8 +491,11 @@ void reap()
     if(!fences.page.cpu)return;
     const uint32_t now=completed();
     while(fences.count && done(fences.ring[fences.head].seq,now)) {
-        Phase phase("reap-free");
-        radv_ps5_memory_free(&fences.ring[fences.head].staging);
+        // A spare for a later submission, which takes it with no kernel call; freed past the spares' budget (staging.h).
+        if(!ps5_gpu_staging_give(&fences.staging,fences.ring[fences.head].staging)) {
+            Phase phase("reap-free");
+            staging_free(fences.ring[fences.head].staging);++staging_refused;
+        }
         fences.ring[fences.head]=Inflight{};fences.head=(fences.head+1)%Fences::kRing;--fences.count;
     }
     for(unsigned i=0;i<fences.deferred_count;)
@@ -503,9 +516,11 @@ bool drain(uint64_t timeout_ns)
 }
 // Held only to publish or take back the fence page, never across a wait: ps5_gpu_broker_health reads the page under it.
 pthread_mutex_t fence_page_lock=PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;
-// After a drain: the fence page goes back, and the state is as at start. In place, as Fences is about 100 KiB.
+// After a drain: the spare staging and the fence page go back, and the state is as at start. In place, as Fences is
+// about 140 KiB.
 void fences_reset()
 {
+    while(fences.staging.count)staging_free(fences.staging.spare[--fences.staging.count]);
     pthread_mutex_lock(&fence_page_lock);
     radv_ps5_memory page=fences.page;
     fences.~Fences();new (&fences) Fences();
@@ -552,9 +567,11 @@ int submit(Client &c,const PwGpuMessage &m,FILE *out,unsigned client)
     }
     const uint64_t t1=radv_ps5_now_ns();c.time_wait+=t1-t0;
     radv_ps5_memory staging{};
-    {
+    if(ps5_gpu_staging_take(&fences.staging,m.bytes+32,&staging))++staging_reuses;
+    else {
         Phase phase("stage-alloc");
-        if(!radv_ps5_memory_alloc(m.bytes+32,16384,false,&staging))return ENOMEM;
+        if(!radv_ps5_memory_alloc(ps5_gpu_staging_round(m.bytes+32),16384,false,&staging))return ENOMEM;
+        ++staging_allocs;staging_held+=staging.bytes;if(staging_held>staging_peak)staging_peak=staging_held;
     }
     {
         // The client's command words, in memory it shares with this process.
@@ -576,7 +593,10 @@ int submit(Client &c,const PwGpuMessage &m,FILE *out,unsigned client)
         // An error cannot prove that nothing reached the hardware: the staging stays, and nothing else is freed.
         if(status) { give_up(out,refused ? "test_submit_failure" : "vulkan_submit_error");return EIO; }
     }
-    if(status) { radv_ps5_memory_free(&staging);return status; }
+    if(status) {   // never submitted: a spare again, or freed past the budget
+        if(!ps5_gpu_staging_give(&fences.staging,staging)) { Phase freeing("stage-free");staging_free(staging);++staging_refused; }
+        return status;
+    }
     fences.ring[(fences.head+fences.count++)%Fences::kRing]=Inflight{seq,staging};
     c.time_submit+=radv_ps5_now_ns()-t2;++c.submissions;++c.waits;
     const bool final=true;
@@ -1214,6 +1234,9 @@ bool ps5_gpu_broker_stop()
         (unsigned long long)fences.kick_ns/1000,wedged);
     // Wedged, the fence page, staging and the reserved ranges (with whatever is mapped in them) stay as they are.
     if(!wedged) { fences_reset();host_ranges_release(b.log); }
+    // Made and freed equal after a clean stop; reuses near the submissions while allocations stay few.
+    fprintf(b.log,"gpu-broker staging allocs=%llu frees=%llu reuses=%llu refused=%llu peak_bytes=%llu held_bytes=%llu\n",
+        staging_allocs,staging_frees,staging_reuses,staging_refused,staging_peak,staging_held);
     if(dmem_alloc)fprintf(b.log,"gpu-broker dmem_host made=%llu freed=%llu refused=%llu free_failed=%llu made_bytes=%llu\n",dmem_made,dmem_freed,dmem_refused,dmem_free_failed,dmem_bytes_made);
     if(b.compositing) {
         pthread_mutex_lock(&b.display_lock);b.compositor_stop=true;pthread_mutex_unlock(&b.display_lock);
diff --git a/tooling/gpu-broker/staging.h b/tooling/gpu-broker/staging.h
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..884a683
--- /dev/null
+++ b/tooling/gpu-broker/staging.h
@@ -0,0 +1,77 @@
+/* Copyright (C) 2026 Mihawk-99 */
+/* SPDX-License-Identifier: LGPL-2.1-or-later */
+#ifndef PS5_GPU_STAGING_H
+#define PS5_GPU_STAGING_H
+#include "radv_ps5_platform.h"
+#include <string.h>
+
+/* The broker's spare staging buffers: owner-private direct memory a submission's command words are copied into, kept
+ * once the fence has passed the submission that used it and taken again by a later one, with no kernel call.
+ *
+ * A 2026-10-16 run: the broker made one staging buffer per submission and freed it once its fence passed, about
+ * 420 of each a second (fences 6842 to 8100 from flight-recorder t=30 to t=33). At t=34 one of those frees (phase
+ * reap-free) did not return for 15 s with every fence done (8125/8125), in the seconds the game's helper held Wine's
+ * virtual mutex and its direct-memory lock inside sceKernelMprotect of direct memory.
+ *
+ * Contract (the caller, tooling/gpu-broker/server.cpp, makes and frees the memory; this is bookkeeping only):
+ *   - Only a buffer no submission in flight can use is given back: its fence done, or never submitted.
+ *   - A buffer is made ps5_gpu_staging_round(need) bytes (radv_ps5_memory_alloc keeps a size that is a multiple of its
+ *     16 KiB page), and a submission takes only a spare of exactly that size. So a buffer is made only when every one
+ *     of its size is in flight (per size, never more than were once in flight together), and a small submission never
+ *     holds a large buffer. Taking the smallest spare that fits instead let small submissions hold the large ones, so
+ *     the next large submission made another: in PS5_Proton's host model (tests/test_pw_gpu_staging.c) the spares
+ *     grew to about 262 MiB in 10 minutes and 279 MiB in an hour, 14 of them 18 MiB, held until the broker stopped.
+ *   - The spares hold at most PS5_GPU_STAGING_BUDGET bytes: give refuses a buffer past it, and the caller frees it.
+ *   - Otherwise spares are freed only where the broker gives its fence page back (fences_reset). */
+enum { PS5_GPU_STAGING_SPARES = 512 };
+/* The smallest buffer: about 4 KiB a submission on average in a 2026-10-16 run (the helper's radv/ps5 line: 2350
+ * submissions, 2442 kwords). Powers of two up to 2 MiB, then 2 MiB steps (direct memory is 2 MiB-aligned from there). */
+#define PS5_GPU_STAGING_MIN UINT64_C(0x10000)
+#define PS5_GPU_STAGING_LARGE UINT64_C(0x200000)
+/* 32 MiB: the fence ring's 512 at the smallest size, or one buffer for the largest submission (16 MiB and the fence
+ * packet, 18 MiB) and 14 MiB of others. The host model's spares held 25.3 MiB after 10 minutes and 26.8 MiB after an
+ * hour (51 and 53 buffers, one of them 18 MiB), none refused. */
+#define PS5_GPU_STAGING_BUDGET (PS5_GPU_STAGING_SPARES * PS5_GPU_STAGING_MIN)
+struct ps5_gpu_staging {
+    struct radv_ps5_memory spare[PS5_GPU_STAGING_SPARES];
+    unsigned count;
+    uint64_t bytes;
+};
+
+static inline uint64_t ps5_gpu_staging_round(uint64_t need)
+{
+    if (need > PS5_GPU_STAGING_LARGE)
+        return (need + PS5_GPU_STAGING_LARGE - 1) & ~(PS5_GPU_STAGING_LARGE - 1);
+    uint64_t bytes = PS5_GPU_STAGING_MIN;
+    while (bytes < need)
+        bytes <<= 1;
+    return bytes;
+}
+
+/* A spare of need's rounded size, taken out into *out: 1. None: 0 (*out zeroed), and the caller makes one. */
+static inline int ps5_gpu_staging_take(struct ps5_gpu_staging *pool, uint64_t need, struct radv_ps5_memory *out)
+{
+    const uint64_t bytes = ps5_gpu_staging_round(need);
+    memset(out, 0, sizeof(*out));
+    for (unsigned i = 0; i < pool->count; ++i) {
+        if (pool->spare[i].bytes != bytes)
+            continue;
+        *out = pool->spare[i];
+        pool->bytes -= bytes;
+        pool->spare[i] = pool->spare[--pool->count];
+        memset(&pool->spare[pool->count], 0, sizeof(pool->spare[0]));
+        return 1;
+    }
+    return 0;
+}
+
+/* A buffer no submission can use any more, kept: 1. 0 when keeping it would pass the budget; the caller frees it. */
+static inline int ps5_gpu_staging_give(struct ps5_gpu_staging *pool, struct radv_ps5_memory memory)
+{
+    if (pool->count == PS5_GPU_STAGING_SPARES || pool->bytes + memory.bytes > PS5_GPU_STAGING_BUDGET)
+        return 0;
+    pool->spare[pool->count++] = memory;
+    pool->bytes += memory.bytes;
+    return 1;
+}
+#endif
